"""Grade 7 life essentials: feelings, the internet and friendship."""
from spec import L, U

M7 = U('Feelings, friends and the internet', 'Morality', 'How do I handle big feelings, stay safe and kind online, and be a good friend?', [
L('Big feelings: sad, scared, angry, and what to do with them',
  'Around twelve, feelings get stronger and less predictable. Many students think having a hard feeling means something is wrong with them or with their faith. The Catechism says passions and feelings are not good or bad in themselves; what matters is how we guide them. The lesson gives students a way to name feelings, pray with them, and ask for help.',
  '1762-1770,1767-1768,2288,1500-1501,2563,2730',
  'Psalm 13;Psalm 42:5-11;Mark 14:32-36;1 Peter 5:7',
  'virtue prayer peace hope',
  'What feeling has been hardest for you lately, and what do you usually do with it?',
  'Draw a “feelings wheel” with six feelings (sad, angry, scared, lonely, jealous, ashamed). For each write one way your body shows it, one wise thing to do, and one person to tell. Then write a four-line prayer from Psalm 13 in your own words: complaint, request, trust.',
  'psalm-23',
  id='7-L1',
  hook='Read Mark 14:32–36 aloud: Jesus in Gethsemane is “greatly distressed and troubled” and says, “My soul is very sorrowful, even to death.” Ask: “Does Jesus hide the feeling? What does he do with it?” (He tells his friends and prays.)',
  teach=[
    'Feelings, which the Catechism calls “passions,” are part of being human. “In themselves passions are neither good nor evil” (CCC 1767). They become good or bad depending on whether reason and will guide them (CCC 1768–1770). So being angry, sad or afraid is not a sin. Jesus himself felt sorrow, anger and fear, even to sweating blood in the garden (Mark 14:33–36; Luke 22:44). If God took a human heart, then feelings are part of how he meets us.',
    'The Bible gives us a language for them. The Psalms are full of complaints that end in trust: “How long, O Lord? Will you forget me forever?... But I have trusted in your steadfast love” (Psalm 13). Praying a psalm is a way to bring the real feeling to God without pretending. The Catechism says prayer is “the raising of one’s mind and heart to God” (CCC 2559), and it includes bringing our whole heart (CCC 2563).',
    'Body and soul go together. “Life and physical health are precious gifts entrusted to us by God. We must take reasonable care of them” (CCC 2288). Sleep, food, exercise, time outside and friends really change how we feel. If a feeling is very heavy, lasts for weeks, or comes with thoughts of hurting yourself, that is not weakness; it is a sign you need help. Tell a parent, a teacher, a school counselor or a priest right away.',
    'Peter says, “Cast all your anxieties on him, for he cares for you” (1 Peter 5:7). That is a practice: name the feeling, tell God, tell a trusted person, do the next small helpful thing. Feelings do not have to be fixed instantly. They pass more easily when we do not hide them.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Why do we sometimes hide feelings? What does hiding do?',
    'Did you know Jesus felt fear and sadness? How does that change how you pray?',
    'What helps your body calm down (breathing, a walk, music, a friend)?',
    'Who are the trusted adults you could tell?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say about feelings (passions)?|They are sins|In themselves they are neither good nor evil|They should be ignored|They are only for the weak|1|CCC 1767.',
    'What can you do with a hard feeling?|Hide it|Name it, tell God, tell a trusted person, do a small helpful step|Get even|Wait for it to disappear|1|1 Peter 5:7; CCC 2563.',
    'When should you tell an adult right away?|Never|If a feeling is very heavy, lasts a long time, or you think of hurting yourself|Only on weekends|Only if friends agree|1|CCC 2288.',
  ],
  life='This week, pick one feeling that has been hard. Each night write one sentence about it and one sentence of prayer. Practise one calming habit (slow breathing, a short walk, music). Tell one trusted adult how you have been feeling.',
  saint='Kateri Tekakwitha: a young Mohawk-Algonquin woman who lost her family to smallpox as a child, was left with scars, was shunned for her faith, and still lived with quiet joy.',
  misc=[
    '“Good Christians do not feel sad or angry.”',
    '“If I tell someone, I am a burden.”',
    '“Feelings are wrong, so I have to get rid of them.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:suffering'],
  mem='“Cast all your anxieties on him, for he cares for you.” (1 Peter 5:7)',
  assess='Ask each student to explain that feelings are not sins, to name two things they do with a hard feeling, and to name a trusted adult.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read Mark 14:32–36 slowly. Ask, “How does Jesus feel? Does he hide it? Who does he tell? What does he do?” Write the answers on the board: he feels it, he tells his friends, he prays.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Church has a word for feelings: passions. The Catechism says passions are neither good nor evil in themselves. Anger, fear, sadness: they are signals. They become good or bad by what we do with them. So having a hard feeling does not mean something is wrong with your faith.”',
      'Say: “Jesus felt sorrow and fear in the garden. He did not pretend. He told Peter, James and John, and he prayed. That is a pattern: feel it, tell someone, pray. The Psalms do the same. Psalm 13 begins ‘How long, O Lord?’ and ends with trust. You can pray the real feeling.”',
      'Say: “Body and soul go together. Sleep, food, exercise and friends change how we feel. The Catechism says we must take reasonable care of our health. If a feeling is very heavy, lasts for weeks, or you think about hurting yourself, tell an adult right away. That is courage, not weakness. Getting help is part of how God cares for us.”',
      'Say: “Peter says, ‘Cast all your anxieties on him.’ It is something you do: name the feeling, tell God, tell a trusted person, do the next small thing. You don’t have to fix it today. Feelings pass more easily when they are not hidden.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What happens when you hide a feeling for a long time?',
      'What helps your body calm down?',
      'Who are two adults you could tell?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Draw a feelings wheel with six feelings.',
      'For each, write: how my body shows it, one wise thing to do, one person to tell.',
      'Write a four-line prayer: complaint, request, trust.',
    ],
    close='Pray Psalm 23 together slowly. Say, “You are not alone with any feeling.”'),
  tryit=['Draw a feelings wheel with six feelings.', 'Write one wise thing and one person for each.', 'Write a four-line psalm prayer.'],
  saintStory='Tekakwitha was born in 1656 in a Mohawk village in what is now New York State. Her father was Mohawk and her mother an Algonquin who was a Christian. When she was four, a smallpox epidemic killed her parents and her baby brother. She survived, but the disease left her face scarred and her eyesight weak. She was raised by an uncle and aunts, who were not Christians.\nWhen she was about nineteen, a Jesuit missionary, Father Jacques de Lamberville, came to the village. She asked to be baptized, and was on Easter Sunday 1676. She took the name Kateri, Catherine. Many in the village did not accept her faith; she was mocked, threatened, and refused food on Sundays because she would not work. In 1677 she walked about 200 miles to a Christian Native settlement near Montreal.\nThere she lived a life of prayer and care for the sick. She died on April 17, 1680, at 24. Witnesses said that the scars on her face disappeared after death. She was canonized in 2012, the first Native American saint.',
),

L('Online: being wise, kind and safe',
  'Students at twelve and thirteen live partly online, and are often handed the tools before the wisdom. The Church’s teaching on dignity, truth, scandal, modesty and prudence is not a list of don’ts; it is a way to be a person online who is wise, kind and safe, and to know what to do when something goes wrong.',
  '1700-1706,2464-2470,2284-2287,2521-2523,1806,2223,2493-2499',
  'Proverbs 14:15;Matthew 10:16;Ephesians 4:29;Luke 6:31',
  'dignity prudence virtue conscience',
  'What is something you saw or were sent online that you wished you had not? What did you do?',
  'Make a personal “online rules card” with five rules in three categories: wise (privacy, passwords, who you talk to), kind (what you post and share), and safe (what to do if something goes wrong). Write the name of one adult you will always tell. Agree on one rule with your family.',
  'guardian-angel',
  id='7-L2',
  hook='Ask: “If you posted a photo on a big screen in the town square, who would see it? How long would it stay? Could you take it back?” Then say, “That is what posting is like.”',
  teach=[
    'Start with dignity. Every person is made in God’s image (CCC 1700–1702) and deserves respect, online as much as offline. A screen can make us forget that there is a real person at the other end. The Golden Rule applies: “As you wish that others would do to you, do so to them” (Luke 6:31). Ask before you post anything about someone else, and do not share anything that would embarrass them.',
    'Prudence is the virtue of looking before you leap: “the wise man looks where he is going” (CCC 1806). Online it means: keep personal information private; use strong passwords; do not talk privately with strangers; remember that anything you post or send can be copied and kept; and do not share private images of yourself or others. Jesus says, “Be wise as serpents and innocent as doves” (Matthew 10:16).',
    'Truth and kindness: the eighth commandment forbids lies, rumors and cruelty (CCC 2464–2470). Scandal, leading others into wrongdoing, can happen through a post or a share (CCC 2284–2287). The Catechism notes that the media should serve truth, justice and solidarity, and that users have a duty to be thoughtful (CCC 2493–2499). Modesty also covers what we share about ourselves: it “protects the mystery of persons and their love” (CCC 2521).',
    'When something goes wrong, tell. Parents have the primary responsibility for guiding children in these matters (CCC 2223), and you can ask them for help without being punished for the problem. If anyone online asks you for pictures, secrecy, or to meet, or if someone is threatening or bullying you, stop, save evidence, and tell a trusted adult right away. That is not tattling; it is exactly what the Church would want.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between private and public online?',
    'Why do people say things online they would never say to someone’s face?',
    'What should you do if someone asks you to keep an online conversation a secret?',
    'What is one rule your family could agree on?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is prudence?|Being afraid|Looking carefully where you are going and choosing the right means|Avoiding all technology|Being clever|1|CCC 1806.',
    'Why is it wise not to share private images?|It is not|They can be copied and kept, and they harm dignity|Only teachers care|Because phones are slow|1|CCC 1806, 2521.',
    'What should you do if an online stranger asks for secrecy?|Keep the secret|Stop and tell a trusted adult|Delete everything and say nothing|Ask friends|1|CCC 2223; Matthew 10:16.',
  ],
  life='Sit down this week with a parent or guardian and show them one app you use. Agree on one rule: for example, phones out of the bedroom at night, or no private chats with people you do not know in person. Write the rule on your card.',
  saint='Isidore of Seville: a scholar-bishop who tried to gather all human knowledge in one book, and is often called the patron of the internet.',
  misc=[
    '“If I delete it, it is gone.”',
    '“Online does not count.”',
    '“Telling an adult will get me in trouble.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-moral'],
  mem='“Be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.” (Matthew 10:16)',
  assess='Ask each student to show their online rules card and explain one rule for each of wise, kind and safe.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask, “If you put a photo on a giant screen in the town square, who would see it? How long would it stay? Could you take it back?” Let them answer. Say, “That is what posting is like.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Church says every person has dignity because they are made in God’s image. That includes the person on the other side of the screen. It is easy to forget when you can’t see a face. The Golden Rule applies: treat others how you want to be treated. Ask before you post about someone.”',
      'Say: “Prudence is the virtue of looking where you are going. The Catechism says the wise man looks where he is going. Online: keep personal information private, use strong passwords, don’t talk privately with strangers, remember everything can be saved and copied, and never share private pictures. Jesus said, ‘Be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.’”',
      'Say: “Truth and kindness: do not spread lies, rumours or cruelty. A post can lead other people to do wrong. That is called scandal. And modesty applies to what we share about ourselves; it protects the mystery of who you are. Not everything needs an audience.”',
      'Say: “If something goes wrong, tell. Your parents are the primary guides. Ask for help without being afraid of punishment. If someone online asks for pictures, secrecy, or to meet, or if you are being threatened or bullied, stop, save the evidence, and tell a trusted adult right away. That is not tattling. It is what the Church would want.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What is something people share online that you think they will regret?',
      'What would you want a friend to do if they saw you in trouble online?',
      'Who is the adult you would tell?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each student a rules card with three headings: Wise, Kind, Safe.',
      'Write five rules in total. Add the name of one adult you would always tell.',
      'Choose one rule to agree on with your family this week.',
    ],
    close='Pray the guardian angel prayer together. Say, “Be wise, be kind, be safe. And if in doubt, tell.”'),
  tryit=['Write five rules: wise, kind, safe.', 'Name the adult you will tell.', 'Pick one rule to agree on at home.'],
  saintStory='Isidore was born around 560 in Cartagena, Spain. His family was driven from the city by invasions, and settled in Seville. His older brother Leander, the archbishop of Seville, raised him, and was a strict teacher. Isidore was a reluctant student, but he is said to have been struck by a worn stone beside a well, hollowed out by the rope that drew water, and he decided that steady effort could wear down anything.\nHe became archbishop of Seville around 600, after Leander died. At a time when the Roman world was fragmenting, and the Visigoths now ruled Spain, he set out to save what could be saved. He founded schools, encouraged study of Greek and Latin, and wrote the Etymologies, a twenty-volume encyclopedia that tried to explain the origin of every word and every field of knowledge. It became one of the most-copied books of the Middle Ages.\nHe died on April 4, 636. In 1722 he was named a Doctor of the Church. He is often called the patron of the internet and of computer users, because his work tried to gather all human knowledge in one place.',
),

L('Friends: the good, the bad, and the one who is left out',
  'Friendships at this age shift quickly. People are dropped, groups form, someone gets left out, and loyalty is tested. The Church’s teaching on friendship, charity and solidarity offers concrete help. A good friend wants your good, tells you the truth, is faithful, and includes. This lesson helps students recognize all four, spot the opposite, and take a small step toward someone who is alone.',
  '1822-1829,2347,1939-1942,1878-1879,2478,1829',
  'Proverbs 17:17;Proverbs 27:6;Ecclesiastes 4:9-12;Luke 10:25-37;John 15:12-15',
  'charity solidarity virtue dignity',
  'What is the difference between a friend and someone who is just fun to be around?',
  'Make a “friend check” chart with four columns: wants my good, tells me the truth, is faithful, includes others. Write one example of each from your own life (or one you wish for). Then write the name of one person who is left out and one specific kind thing you could do this week.',
  'st-francis',
  id='7-L3',
  hook='Read Luke 10:25–37 (the Good Samaritan) in a few lines. Ask: “Who walked past? Who stopped? What did the one who stopped pay?” Then ask: “Who is the person nobody stops for in our school?”',
  teach=[
    'The Catechism calls friendship “a great good for all” (CCC 2347). We are made for one another; “man is a social being” (CCC 1879). And the deepest form of friendship is charity, loving the other for their sake (CCC 1822–1829). Jesus says, “No longer do I call you servants... but I have called you friends” (John 15:15). A friend is someone who wants your good and shows it.',
    'Four marks of a true friend, from Scripture and the tradition. A true friend wants your good, not just what you can give (Proverbs 17:17). A true friend tells you the truth gently: “Faithful are the wounds of a friend” (Proverbs 27:6). A true friend is faithful, there in hard times (Ecclesiastes 4:9–12). A true friend includes others and does not use friendship as a weapon. If someone makes you do things you know are wrong to keep their friendship, that is not friendship.',
    'The Good Samaritan shows a wider friendship. Jesus answers “Who is my neighbor?” with a story of a stranger. The Church calls this solidarity, which the Catechism also describes as “friendship” or “social charity”: we are responsible for one another (CCC 1939–1942). In school, that looks like noticing the one who eats alone and sitting with them. It costs a little and means a lot.',
    'When a friendship goes wrong, treat it as a moral matter. Do not spread what they told you in confidence; do not gossip (CCC 2477–2478). If you must end a friendship, be honest and kind. If you are being used or bullied, tell an adult. And if you have hurt a friend, say sorry. A good friend is someone you can be honest with and still be loved.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Which of the four marks of a friend is hardest for you to find? To give?',
    'What makes people leave someone out? What would help?',
    'What does it cost to sit with someone who is alone?',
    'How can you end a friendship kindly?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say friendship is?|A waste of time|A great good for all|Only for adults|A reward|1|CCC 2347.',
    'What are four marks of a true friend?|Fun, popular, funny, rich|Wants your good, tells the truth, faithful, includes others|Always agrees, never argues|Does what you want|1|Proverbs 17:17; 27:6; Ecclesiastes 4:9–12.',
    'What is solidarity?|A club|The principle that we are responsible for one another, also called friendship or social charity|A rule|A holiday|1|CCC 1939–1942.',
  ],
  life='This week, pick one person who is often alone or left out. Do one specific small thing: sit with them at lunch, say hello by name, include them in a game. Do it again the next day. Then ask yourself one question: am I a friend with all four marks?',
  saint='Basil the Great and Gregory of Nazianzus: friends since their school days in Athens, who later became bishops and teachers of the Church.',
  misc=[
    '“A real friend always agrees with me.”',
    '“Including others will cost me my friends.”',
    '“If a friend asks me to keep a harmful secret, I have to.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-confessions'],
  mem='“A friend loves at all times.” (Proverbs 17:17)',
  assess='Ask each student to name the four marks of a friend, rate one friendship honestly against them, and name the person they will include this week.',
  script=dict(
    open='Retell the Good Samaritan in two minutes. Ask, “Who walked past? Who stopped? What did stopping cost?” Then ask, quietly, “Who in our school is the person nobody stops for?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “The Church says friendship is a great good. We are made for each other. Jesus says, ‘I have called you friends.’ But what is a real friend? Four marks. A true friend wants your good, not just what you can give. A true friend tells you the truth kindly. A true friend is faithful, there in hard times. And a true friend includes others. It does not use friendship as a weapon.”',
      'Say: “Proverbs says, ‘A friend loves at all times,’ and ‘Faithful are the wounds of a friend.’ That means a good friend will sometimes say something hard. If a friend only flatters you, or makes you do things you know are wrong to keep the friendship, that is not friendship, it is pressure.”',
      'Say: “The Good Samaritan is Jesus’ answer to ‘Who is my neighbor?’ A stranger who stopped. The Church calls it solidarity: we are responsible for one another. At school it looks small: sitting with someone who is alone, saying a name, inviting someone to join.”',
      'Say: “When a friendship goes wrong, it is a moral matter. Don’t spread secrets. Don’t gossip. If you have to end it, do it honestly and kindly. If you are being used or bullied, tell an adult. If you hurt a friend, say sorry. A good friend is someone you can be honest with and still be loved.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Who has been a true friend to you? Which marks did they show?',
      'Who is left out in your school or group? What small step could you take?',
      'What would you want a friend to say to you if you were making a mistake?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out a friend check chart with the four marks across the top.',
      'Write one example of each mark from your life, or one you wish for.',
      'Write the name of one person to include this week and what you will do.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer of St. Francis together. Say, “Be the friend you want to find.”'),
  tryit=['Fill in the four marks with examples.', 'Name one person to include.', 'Plan one small kind act.'],
  saintStory='Basil was born around 330 in Caesarea in Cappadocia, in what is now Turkey, to a wealthy and devout family. Gregory was born about a year earlier in nearby Nazianzus, the son of a bishop. They met as students in Athens in the 350s, which was then the great university of the Greek world.\nGregory later wrote about their friendship. He said that they became so close that it seemed they were one soul in two bodies. They shared the same two roads in Athens, the road to church and the road to the school of the teachers. They encouraged one another to study hard and to live purely at a time when many students drank and caroused.\nAfterward, they lived for a time as monks together, and later both became bishops. They had sharp disagreements and hurt each other at times; Basil once placed Gregory in a small, unattractive town as bishop, and Gregory resented it. They drifted apart, but remained deeply attached. Basil died in 379. Gregory went on to speak in defense of the Trinity at Constantinople, and died in 390. Both are Doctors of the Church.',
),
])
